/**

@mainpage


The NDS (Nominal Device Support) Core library gives the developer the possibility to organize its device support in a tree-like structure, with one or more NDS nodes, according to the functionality to be implemented. 
These NDS nodes cover state machines, data acquisition, waveform generation, timing, data processing, digital acquisition, image acquisition, DMA acquisition, health monitoring, streaming, 
and firmware control. As many nodes as needed can be added to each device. NDS also includes the possibility to add PVs to support extra functionality.
NDS uses standard C++ types and UNIX EPOCH timing to communicate with the library. NDS Device Drivers need an underlying control system in charge of implementing the interface and the 
management of the device driver. The user manual presents two possible implementations of the control system: a primary control system for test purposes and the interface with EPICS control 
system using an additional software module called NDS EPICS.

NDS provides the following features:

- organizes your device in a tree-like structure using nodes.
- provides the data pull mode (passive scanning on EPICS) and the push mode (Interrupt
  on EPICS )
- your code uses standard C++ types and Unix EPOCH timing to communicate with the library
- supplies an hierarchical state machine
- supplies a collection of specialized data acquisition, waveform generation and timing nodes.


*/
